Found while writing the karr #92 regression test.

Langertha::Response overloads only stringification:

  lib/Langertha/Response.pm:8-10
    use overload
      "\"\"" => sub { $_[0]->content },
      fallback => 1;

With fallback => 1 Perl derives bool from the "" overload, so a Response whose
content is the empty string is FALSE in boolean context. That is exactly the
shape of a tool-call-only reply (Ollama fixture t/data/ollama_tool_call_response.json
has message.content == "" plus one tool_call), and of Anthropic replies that are
pure tool_use.

Consequence for callers:

  my $resp = $engine->simple_chat(...);
  if ($resp) { ... }            # silently skipped for a tool-call-only reply
  $resp or die "no response";   # dies on a perfectly good Response

It bit the new test itself: ok($resp, ...) failed on a successfully constructed
object; the test now asserts defined-ness instead.

Fix candidates (design decision, not a drive-by):
  a) add bool => sub { 1 } to the overload list (an object that exists is true)
  b) leave it and document the trap in the Response POD SYNOPSIS

(a) is the honest one and matches how every other value object in the dist
behaves, but it is a public-behavior change: code doing `if ($resp)` as a
stand-in for `if (length $resp)` would flip. Worth deciding deliberately.
Decision: option (a) — added 'bool' => sub { 1 } to the overload list.

Scope widened by one file after the consistency check: Langertha::Result has the
identical pattern ("" => text // '', fallback => 1), and there it is worse —
question/pause/abort carry their message in 'content', not 'text', so EVERY result
a caller must actually handle was false. Langertha::Raider::Result inherits the fix.
Langertha::Usage (%{} overload), ToolCall and Stream::Chunk were already
true-because-they-exist (verified empirically), so this makes the dist consistent
rather than introducing a new convention.

Second case the ticket did not name: content '0' was false too — the classic Perl
false-string trap, hit by any model answering a bare count or a JSON 0.

Back-compat audit: nothing in lib/, bin/ or t/ relies on the falseness. Every
boolean test on a Langertha::Response/Result goes through a predicate (has_*,
is_success on HTTP::Response, is_final/is_question) or through ref/blessed + isa.
The two live tests that combine them (t/86_live_prompt_cache.t:94 and
t/86_live_reasoning_effort.t) already spell the content check as length($resp),
which is unaffected.

Stringification contract untouched: "$r" is still content/text, and eq/ne/concat
keep routing through the "" overload — asserted explicitly, and those assertions
pass both before and after, so they are not vacuous.

Documented as '=head2 Boolean context' in both classes' POD, including the spelling
for the question callers actually mean (length "$resp" / has_tool_calls / has_text).

Verification (offline, no live calls): red first — 5 of 14 new assertions in
t/70_response.t and 6 in t/85_raider_result.t fail against unpatched HEAD.
Green after: isolated HEAD+patch tree, prove -lr t/ = 85 files / 2675 tests pass
(baseline 2663 + 12 new). perlcritic --profile .perlcriticrc lib/ bin/ maint/ clean.
Not committed.
